course: dictionary
lesson: 13167
title: Fossilised: old as stone
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Context means the words around a word, and they often hold clues to its meaning. «The scientist found a fossilised bone in the rock.» The word rock tells you the bone was buried for a long time, so fossilised must have something to do with stone and very long ago.
explain: «The fossilised footprints were left by dinosaurs millions of years ago.» Millions of years is a huge amount of time. A footprint that lasts that long has slowly turned to stone, which is what fossilised means.
explain: Words can also be used in a different way. «Her ideas are fossilised; she will not try anything new.» Ideas cannot turn to stone, so this is a figure of speech. It means her ideas are old-fashioned and stuck, just like something that has not changed for ages.
fact: Fossilised means turned into a fossil. A fossil is the remains or print of a plant or animal that has slowly turned to stone.
fact: Fossils can take millions of years to form, so a fossilised thing is usually very, very old.
fact: «Fossilised» can also describe ideas or rules that are old-fashioned and never change. The US spelling is fossilized.
fact: «Fossilised» is used in science and history lessons, for example fossilised bones, fossilised trees, fossilised footprints and fossilised poo, which scientists call coprolite.
fact: A good check is to swap in a simple meaning. If «turned to stone long ago» fits the sentence, you have understood the word.
exercise: «The scientist found a fossilised bone in the rock.» What is a fossilised bone? | *An old bone that has slowly turned to stone | A bone that someone has just painted | A bone from a fish that was caught today
exercise: «The fossilised footprints were left by dinosaurs millions of years ago.» Who made the footprints? | *Dinosaurs | Children on a school trip | Birds in the park | Dogs on a walk
exercise: «Her ideas are fossilised; she will not try anything new.» What does fossilised mean here? | *Old-fashioned and unable to change | Very shiny and new | Turned into a fossil at the beach | Melted by the sun
test: «The museum has a fossilised tree in the hall.» What is the best meaning of fossilised here? | Cut into small pieces | Broken in a storm | *Turned to stone over a very long time | Painted bright green
test: Grandma says the school rules are fossilised. What does she mean? | The rules are fun and easy to follow | The rules were written last week | *The rules are old-fashioned and never change | The rules are written in gold
exam: What do fossilised ideas mean? | Ideas that are written in pencil | Ideas that are brand new every day | *Ideas that never change and stay stuck in the past | Ideas that a fish had
